**New evidence**
I kept the new set focused on direct program articulation, implementation, and outcome evidence.

- PM’s address on Union Budget 2022-23, Prime Minister of India, 2022-02-01
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM's address on Union Budget 2022-23","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/news_updates/pms-address-on-union-budget-2022-23/","claim":"Modi framed the 2022-23 budget around infrastructure, investment, growth, jobs, green jobs, and welfare measures including pucca housing, tap water, toilets, gas connections, mountain connectivity, and farm income support.","locator":"paragraphs 74-83","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct PMO transcript of the ruler's own public statement; highly reliable for stated priorities but not by itself proof of execution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and tied to the 2022 budget program.","ruler_attribution":"High for revealed priorities; low for implementation on its own because the item is declarative rather than an outcomes record.","contrary_evidence":["The speech does not prove delivery.","Later outcome gaps in jobs and learning show only partial realization."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6"]}

- Union Budget 2022-23 Analysis, PRS Legislative Research, 2022-02-01
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Union Budget 2022-23 Analysis","publisher":"PRS Legislative Research","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://prsindia.org/budgets/parliament/union-budget-2022-23-analysis","claim":"PRS summarized the budget as raising capital expenditure by 24.5 percent to Rs 7,50,246 crore and increasing allocations for roads, Jal Jeevan Mission, National Education Mission, women and child welfare, SC/ST, and the North Eastern Region.","locator":"Budget Highlights; Table 6; tables 7-8; lines 341, 1266-1337","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent legislative research that directly parses the union budget documents and reproduces the main numerical choices.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022-23 budget adopted during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for central budget priorities; weaker for downstream implementation because this is a plan document.","contrary_evidence":["Budgeted allocations are not the same as delivered outcomes.","Some spending lines did not automatically convert into measurable 2022 gains."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- PM addresses post-budget webinar on vision of GatiShakti, Prime Minister of India, 2022-02-28
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM addresses post-budget webinar on vision of 'GatiShakti'","publisher":"Prime Minister of India","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/news_updates/pm-addresses-post-budget-webinar-on-vision-of-gatishakti/","claim":"Modi said PM GatiShakti would change infrastructure planning, implementation, and monitoring; he said more than 400 data layers were available and 24 digital systems from 6 ministries were being integrated through ULIP to reduce logistics costs.","locator":"lines 73-98","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official transcript of the PM's own explanation of the program, strong for strategy and intended mechanism but not independent proof of final performance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within the target year and explicitly tied to Union Budget 2022 and 2022 implementation.","ruler_attribution":"High for strategic direction and coordination model; lower for actual delivery because this is still a declared implementation framework.","contrary_evidence":["The webinar is aspirational and does not itself show completion.","Claims about efficiency still need independent outcome checks."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.5","8B.6"]}

- Shri Piyush Goyal reviews progress of PM GatiShakti National Master Plan, Press Information Bureau, 2022-11-16
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Shri Piyush Goyal reviews progress of PM GatiShakti National Master Plan","publisher":"Press Information Bureau","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1876607&reg=48&lang=2","claim":"By November 2022, PM GatiShakti had incorporated more than 1,900 GIS data layers and over 250 infrastructure projects had been examined in the prior year, with officials saying the system expedited previously stalled projects.","locator":"lines 13-19","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official progress review with concrete counts, but it is still a ministry self-report rather than an independent audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year and reporting 2022 progress since the 2021 launch.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate to strong for the central government program; implementation is shared across ministries and states.","contrary_evidence":["The report is not an external audit.","It does not independently verify project quality or long-term completion."],"lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.8"]}

- Audit Report No. 11 of 2023 - Performance Audit of Ayushman Bharat-Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana, Comptroller and Auditor General of India, 2023-08-08
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Report No. 11 of 2023 - Performance Audit of Ayushman Bharat-Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana","publisher":"Comptroller and Auditor General of India","publication_date":"2023-08-08","url":"https://cag.gov.in/en/audit-report/details/119060","claim":"The audit says 131,150 Health and Wellness Centres were functional as of 30 November 2022, the government approved expansion of PMJAY to 12 crore families in January 2022, and 7.87 crore beneficiary households were registered, about 73 percent of the target.","locator":"overview paragraphs 208-216","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent constitutional audit with concrete operational numbers covering the target period through late 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Audit published in 2023 but explicitly covers implementation through 30 November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for central scheme design and funding; implementation also depends on the National Health Authority and state health systems.","contrary_evidence":["Registration fell short of the target household base.","The audit shows reach, but not full completion."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with India, IMF, 2022-12-19
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with India","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/19/pr22444-india-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation","claim":"The IMF said the authorities responded to post-pandemic shocks with fiscal policy support to vulnerable groups and front-loaded monetary tightening; it also said digital public infrastructure enabled rapid support deployment and financial inclusion, and that real GDP grew 8.7 percent in FY2021/22.","locator":"lines 243, 251, 255, 287","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral surveillance with specific quantitative claims, though some policy levers were RBI-led and some outcomes reflect inherited recovery dynamics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 policy response and FY2021/22 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Strong for fiscal support and digital public infrastructure; mixed for monetary tightening because the RBI is institutionally independent.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation remained elevated and growth was expected to moderate.","Some key levers were shared with or delegated to the RBI rather than controlled solely by Modi."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- India Better Positioned to Navigate Global Headwinds Than Other Major Emerging Economies: New World Bank Report, World Bank, 2022-12-05
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India Better Positioned to Navigate Global Headwinds Than Other Major Emerging Economies: New World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/05/india-better-positioned-to-navigate-global-headwinds-than-other-major-emerging-economies-new-world-bank-report","claim":"The World Bank said India was relatively well positioned to weather global spillovers, that policy reforms and prudent regulatory measures improved resilience, and that market borrowings improved the transparency and credibility of fiscal policy.","locator":"lines 36 and 44","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with clear links between reforms and macro resilience, though some reforms predate 2022 and some outcomes are indirect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and evaluating 2022 conditions and policy response.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate, because the report combines Modi-era reforms, RBI actions, and inherited macro buffers.","contrary_evidence":["External shocks and a widening current-account deficit still weighed on growth.","The report is favorable but not a clean ruler-only causal finding."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- How India's Central Bank Helped Spur a Digital Payments Boom, IMF, 2022-10-27
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"How India's Central Bank Helped Spur a Digital Payments Boom","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/10/26/cf-how-indias-central-bank-helped-spur-a-digital-payments-boom","claim":"The IMF reported that India's digital payment volume rose at about 50 percent annually over five years, UPI transactions more than doubled to 5.86 billion in June, and the system was expanding to feature phones for rural users.","locator":"lines 271-275","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IMF feature with concrete usage figures, but the causal chain is shared by the RBI, NPCI, banks, and private firms rather than Modi alone.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to the government's digitalization program.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect. This is strongest as evidence of a national digital-state program that Modi promoted, but the article ties the mechanism substantially to RBI and ecosystem partners.","contrary_evidence":["The article credits RBI and private firms as much as the central government.","Digital payment growth does not by itself prove broader development gains."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across PMO, PIB, PRS, CAG, IMF, World Bank, NITI, and outcome-data pages, plus duplicate hits from the same themes.
- I kept the sources that best traced the chain from declared program to implementation to outcome: the 2022 budget speech, PRS budget analysis, GatiShakti webinar and progress review, CAG PMJAY audit, IMF Article IV materials, World Bank India update, and IMF digital-payments feature.
- I rejected repeated or redundant pages that said the same thing as a better source already chosen, including overlapping PMO/PIB scheme pages, duplicated NITI appointment pages, and overlapping IMF press-release versus staff-report versions when they did not add a materially distinct claim.
- I rejected blocked or unhelpful material such as the cache-missed budget PDF, 2023/2024 annual reports that did not add new 2022-specific evidence, and 2024/2026 follow-ups that were out of period for the target year.
- I treated broad country-only indicators and integrity-focused material as context unless they clearly connected to Modi’s 2022 program execution. That is why Adani-style reporting was not used here.
- I retained a few adverse outcome series from prior chapter work as contrary context only, mainly for labor-market quality and learning outcomes.

**Remaining gaps**
- The biggest gap is independent evaluation of whether PM GatiShakti actually improved project completion times, logistics costs, or coordination beyond the government’s own progress counts.
- A second gap is cleaner attribution between Modi, the Finance Ministry, the RBI, and state governments on the macro and digital outcomes.
- A third gap is direct evidence on job-quality and labor-market improvement from the 2022 program set, rather than just growth, spending, or digital uptake.
- A fourth gap is stronger independent evidence on whether the 2022 budget priorities produced durable gains rather than one-year administrative motion.
- No score assigned.
